# Catch-all FIRST. In .gitattributes the LAST matching line wins, so a trailing
# `* text=auto` would silently override every specific rule below it — and a CR
# smuggled into a shell script's shebang gives "bad interpreter" on Linux.
#
# `eol=lf` on the catch-all, and it is load bearing rather than tidiness. The
# template manifest records a hash per shipped file, and a file whose bytes
# depend on which platform checked it out cannot have one hash. Without the pin,
# every file named by no specific rule below — a .toml, a .js, LICENSE — checks
# out CRLF under Git for Windows' default core.autocrlf, and every Windows vault
# would be told that files nobody had opened were locally modified. Pinning here
# removes the whole class rather than compensating for it downstream.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF endings even when checked out on Windows, or the
# hooks fail on any machine that actually runs bash.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Git hooks have no extension, so *.sh does not reach them.
.claude/githooks/* text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the mirror image: they need CRLF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Markdown, JSON and YAML are normalised to LF in the repository.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# The version marker and the generated manifest, spelled out even though the
# catch-all above already reaches them. Neither has an extension that any rule
# here names, so before the catch-all carried `eol=lf` the manifest checked out
# CRLF on Windows, every path in it arrived carrying a trailing carriage return,
# and the comparison reported the entire template as both gone and new at once.
# Naming them keeps that reasoning attached to the files it is about.
VERSION                   text eol=lf
.claude/template-manifest text eol=lf
.claude/manifest-rules    text eol=lf
